{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "def max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr, length, i, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from prefix until ith index\n    and also including a given kth element, where k > i.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Input validation for length and indices.\n    if not arr or length <= 0 or not (0 <= i < length) or not (0 <= k < length):\n        return 0\n\n    # The problem explicitly states that k must be greater than i.\n    # \"including a given kth element which is after i, i.e., k > i\"\n    # If k <= i, this condition is not met, so return 0.\n    if k <= i:\n        return 0\n\n    val_k = arr[k]\n\n    # dp_max_ending_at_j[j] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence\n    # ending at arr[j] (where j <= i) such that arr[j] < val_k.\n    # This prepares the part of the subsequence that comes before arr[k].\n    dp_max_ending_at_j = [0] * (i + 1)\n    max_prefix_sum_before_k = 0\n\n    # Iterate through the prefix elements (indices 0 to i)\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        # Only consider elements that can be part of an increasing subsequence ending before arr[k]\n        if arr[j] < val_k:\n            current_sum_ending_at_j = arr[j]\n            # Find the maximum previous sum ending at index p where arr[p] < arr[j]\n            for p in range(j):\n                # dp_max_ending_at_j[p] already ensures arr[p] < val_k and represents a valid sum\n                if arr[p] < arr[j]:\n                    current_sum_ending_at_j = max(current_sum_ending_at_j, dp_max_ending_at_j[p] + arr[j])\n            \n            dp_max_ending_at_j[j] = current_sum_ending_at_j\n            max_prefix_sum_before_k = max(max_prefix_sum_before_k, dp_max_ending_at_j[j])\n        else:\n            # If arr[j] is not less than val_k, it cannot be part of the increasing subsequence\n            # that precedes arr[k], so its contribution is 0.\n            dp_max_ending_at_j[j] = 0\n\n    # The maximum sum increasing subsequence, given k > i, must have arr[k] as its last element.\n    # This is because any element arr[q] from arr[0...i] would have q <= i.\n    # If arr[q] were to come after arr[k] in the subsequence, its index q would have to be > k.\n    # But q <= i and k > i makes q > k impossible.\n    # So, the final sum is the maximum prefix sum (ending before arr[k]) plus arr[k] itself.\n    return max_prefix_sum_before_k + val_k"}
