{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(value: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the most significant unset (0) bit of a non-negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if value == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Determine the number of bits required to represent the value.\n    k = value.bit_length()\n\n    # Create a mask with 'k' set bits (e.g., for value=0b1010, k=4, mask=0b1111).\n    mask = (1 << k) - 1\n\n    # Invert the bits of 'value' within this 'k'-bit segment.\n    # This `inverted_segment` now has '1's at positions where `value` had '0's.\n    inverted_segment = mask ^ value\n\n    # Find the position of the most significant '1' bit in `inverted_segment`.\n    if inverted_segment == 0:\n        # If all bits up to 'k-1' were set in 'value', the leftmost unset bit\n        # is at position 'k'.\n        pos = k\n    else:\n        # Otherwise, the position is the bit_length of the inverted segment minus 1.\n        pos = inverted_segment.bit_length() - 1\n\n    # Set the identified bit in the original value.\n    return value | (1 << pos)"}
